# Part Two — Today's Debates

> The 2024–2026 papers ask about emerging trends, digital activism, bodily autonomy and NGO-isation. These are the live debates of Topic 3. Each gives you facts, both sides and a line to take.

## Debate 1 · The Aurat March: liberation or provocation?

::: debate | The Aurat March
Since ==8 March 2018== the Aurat March has brought thousands of women onto the streets of Karachi, Lahore, Islamabad and other cities with demands on violence, harassment, inheritance, workers' rights, *khawaja sira* rights and bodily autonomy.
- **Supporters:** it revived street feminism after years of NGO-isation; it is self-funded and volunteer-run; it made women's issues national news; its slogans express real grievances — forced marriage, harassment, domestic labour.
- **Critics:** some slogans were seen as vulgar or against religion and culture; it is urban and elite; it hands opponents a weapon to discredit all women's rights.
- **The state and courts:** petitions to ban it were filed; courts generally upheld the right to march under Articles 16 and 17 (assembly and association) while urging "decency".
-> **Line to take:** the march's **substance** — freedom from violence, fair work, inheritance — commands wide support; debate over **slogans** should not hide it. A peaceful march is a constitutional right.
:::

## Debate 2 · Is feminism in Pakistan a Western agenda?

- **The charge:** foreign-funded NGOs and Western-educated leaders import alien values.
- **The reply:** local roots (Mumtaz Ali ==1898==; women in the Pakistan Movement; the Quaid's ==1944== speech); Islamic feminism; rural movements such as the Sindhiani Tehreek; and the fact that the main demands — safety, education, inheritance, fair wages — are rooted in the Constitution and in Islam.
- **Line to take:** tools and platforms came from abroad; the struggle and its reasons are Pakistani (Chapter 9).

## Debate 3 · Has the movement moved "from law to the body" — and is that wise?

The 2026 question claims a shift from **legal rights** to **bodily autonomy**. Some activists argue that without control over their bodies — freedom from violence, forced marriage and harassment — women cannot use legal rights at all. Others fear that bodily-autonomy language, heard as sexual freedom, provokes backlash and loses allies. The strongest answers show that the two are linked: **laws protect bodies**, and the new activism still demands laws.

## Debate 4 · Can digital activism reach rural women?

::: data The digital gap | GSMA as cited in CSS 2026
Pakistan's mobile-internet gender gap fell from ==38%== to ==25%==, with about eight million more women online (2024–2025).
:::
- **Optimists:** cheaper smartphones, WhatsApp and voice notes reach women in villages; helplines offer help without leaving home.
- **Pessimists:** a quarter of the gap remains; men often control family phones; online abuse drives women offline.
- **Line to take:** digital activism must be paired with **offline organising** — Lady Health Workers, community groups and local councillors.

## Debate 5 · NGO-isation: did donors capture the movement?

Covered in Chapter 10. The line to take: partly — but NGOs also won laws and services, and self-funded movements have revived politics. A division of labour between NGOs and movements is the way forward.

## Debate 6 · Beijing+30 and the global backlash

::: update | Beijing+30 (2025)
The thirtieth-anniversary review of the Beijing Platform, held at the UN Commission on the Status of Women in March ==2025==, recorded progress in girls' education, legal reform and women's representation, but warned of **slow change in power and pay**, persistent **violence**, and a **growing backlash against women's rights** and "gender" in many countries. The reversal of *Roe v. Wade* (==2022==) and restrictions on women in Afghanistan since ==2021== are often cited.
-> For Pakistan: international commitments are only as strong as domestic political will.
:::

## Debate 7 · Secular or Islamic feminism — one movement or two?

WAF was secular; many younger and rural activists use Islamic arguments; religious women's organisations run their own campaigns on dowry, inheritance and education. The debate asks whether these can form **one movement**. Scholars such as **Afiya Zia** (*Faith and Feminism in Pakistan*, ==2018==) warn that faith-based approaches may limit reform; others argue that broad alliances are the only way to reach most women. **Line to take:** coalitions on shared goals — violence, inheritance, education, child marriage — are possible without agreeing on everything.

## Debate 8 · Men and the movement

Men marched in the Aurat March, male legislators voted for the laws of 2010–2016, and male scholars have supported inheritance and consent. Yet men also lead the backlash, online and offline. The debate is how to turn men from **gatekeepers** into **allies** (Topic 2, Chapter 6).
